<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ong Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1203">1203</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Toghrul, Wang Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ong Khan, born Toghrul, was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and a sworn brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>'s father. He supported the young Temüjin, the future Genghis Khan, and received the title Ong (Wang) Khan from the Jin dynasty after a joint campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> in 1196. The two later fell out, and Temüjin defeated him in 1203; he was killed while fleeing.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ong-khan.jpg" alt="Ong Khan" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maître d'Egerton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WangKhan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Toghrul was born around 1130 into a Nestorian family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a>, the son of their leader Cyriacus Buyruk Khan. His youth was hard. The Merkits captured him in childhood and reduced him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> abducted him again at thirteen, together with his mother. When he returned, his father was near death, and Toghrul took his place and commanded the Keraites around 1165.</p><p>The name Wang Khan was given to him by the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, wang meaning king or prince. In the 13th century he was one of several Asian leaders identified with the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prester-john">Prester John</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> had converted to Nestorian Christianity early in the 11th century, but there is no credible proof that Toghrul himself was Christian, as Mongolian sources say nothing of his religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>To remove rivals he killed his brothers between 1165 and 1171, but an uncle known as Gurkhan overthrew him. He fled and appealed to Yesugei, the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> and his blood brother, or anda, who attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> and restored him.</p><p>He later led the coalition that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a> and his own brother Jakha Gambhu against the Merkits after Temüjin's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a> was abducted in 1183. In 1194 Temüjin asked his help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, and Toghrul joined him with a few thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a>. The Jin minister Wanyan Xiang also moved against the Tatars, who were caught between the two forces; the men were slaughtered and the children adopted or made servants and slaves. It was at this time that he received the name Wang Khan.</p><p>Soon afterwards he was overthrown by his brother Erke Qara, who returned with Naiman support, and he fled to Yelü Zhilugu. He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>'s camp around 1196 according to Rashid al-Din and stayed about two years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> returned to him around 1198. In 1201 he joined Temüjin against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a>, whom a coalition of tribes had recognised as Gurkhan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 1203 Toghrul was over 70, and his son Ilga Senggüm commanded the Kerait armies. Senggüm, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamukha">Jamukha</a>, Altan and Qutchar, tried to eliminate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a> and failed to assassinate him. In the Battle of the Burning Sands Temüjin fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a>, and when he asked for peace Senggüm rejected it. Temüjin then attacked by surprise, and after three days the Keraites surrendered.</p><p>Toghrul fled to the Naiman ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayang-khan">Tayang Khan</a> but was killed by a Naiman soldier, Qori Sübeči, who did not recognise him. Tayang later noticed the severed head and crushed it underfoot. Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keraites">Keraites</a> allied themselves with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>. From the 15th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torghut">Torghut</a> nobles claimed descent from Toghrul.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toghrul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1203 Temüjin defeated <b>Toghrul</b> and used the flight of Toghrul's son through Tangut land as a pretext to raid Western Xia in 1205; in 1206, the year he became Genghis Khan, Emperor Huanzong was deposed by his cousin Anquan (Xiangzong).</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Toghrul</b>, sworn brother of Temüjin's father, became the most powerful khan of central Mongolia and received the title Ong Khan from the Jin dynasty (1115-1234) in 1196 after the campaign against the Tatars.</blockquote><a href="/state/keraite-khanate">Keraite Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Secret History of the Mongols, Temüjin's father Yesügei had stolen his wife Hö'elün from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride Börte, who was rescued with help from <b>Toghrul</b> of the Keraite Khanate and Jamukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1196 the Jin, with Temüjin and the Keraite ruler <b>Toghrul</b>, defeated the Tatar chief Megüjin Seultü.</blockquote><a href="/state/tatar-confederation">Tatar confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1193" aria-label="Mongolia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ong Khan"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4119 6085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4119 6085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46,105&amp;year=1193">What was here in 1193? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
